#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "f781ef9b-491f-5df4-a273-94f30ce79185" :question/math-academy-id "e-389" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)=(2x-4)^2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a left Riemann sum with $4$ rectangles of equal width." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x$, which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2$, and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/56/56427bec8108b822c4e1980725073cedc9fc79575d606014f72ea6ca9f8959c2.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\approx (16 + 9 + 4 + 1) \\cdot 0.5 = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "6220f2e1-e654-5a30-a0f8-377bb0d64bc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-390" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2]$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2]$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is computed as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(0.0)\\Delta x_1+f(0.8)\\Delta x_2+f(1.6)\\Delta x_3\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i+1}-x_{i}\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Delta{x}$ | $0.8$ | $0.8$ | $0.4$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx 2 \\cdot 0.8 + 5 \\cdot 0.8 + 10 \\cdot 0.4; = 1.6 + 4 + 4; = 9.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "797ec111-8928-5875-8f94-9a423ff558f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7228" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[-4,2]$. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $28$, then what must be the value of $k$?\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula for the left Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-4)}}+{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (5 + 3 + k) \\cdot 2; = (8 + k) \\cdot 2; = 16 + 2k\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $28$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 2k &= 28 \\\\\n2k &= 12 \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "60eae905-fa87-5eac-bff2-8326ae41ca1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7230" :question/problem "Suppose a left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)=(2x-4)^2$ over the interval $[0,2]$. Is the resulting estimation an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/02/02b3449f0ccdb2f60eb489aec1b34485ee381b644a52eea7f5394e16851b6cff.png)\n\nBecause the function is *decreasing* over the interval $[0,2]$, the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e29fb0-b035-5d75-87d7-cd58bd0e6de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b5cce5427550d19bb55a8dcf609e22a8a45be1f2c44285ec97c9b70278616171.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067427} {:answer/id #uuid "da3748ad-eb6b-5657-b64a-dde5ec7d568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f70e633c3881bf78a2652141979ba9f87b95744a896b9ce11fca9d6f3af9d716.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067428} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0ef57-6891-55fa-98c4-2c63fecf7247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/376b43aca96daddb245dcc5f14f655ee726848370dbf6a11db617c7e88aba597.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067429} {:answer/id #uuid "13849db4-23b6-5fcb-abf2-ba531c40c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c0654783dff2b22fcf834e37c8b6872a262871971438e0cdc7a6e3399684220.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067430} {:answer/id #uuid "7525a1f5-f389-5bbe-81be-be2f8b230f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067431}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4794cc-e7f3-589f-8563-a0062f45afe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067426}] :question/id #uuid "965cb62a-f834-5614-8e95-ef5ea2ae941c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49109" :question/problem "A left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y = f(x)$ between $x =-1$ and $x = 3$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
